-### Parameters
-*   **Positional Encoding Frequencies:** 10 for $\mathbf{x}$, 4 for $\mathbf{d}$ [S2].
-*   **Optimizer:** AdamW [S2].
-*   **Learning Rates (Training):**
-    *   Network parameters $\Theta$: $1 \times 10^{-4}$ [S2].
-    *   Latent vectors $\mathbf{z_s}, \mathbf{z_t}$: $1 \times 10^{-3}$ [S2].
-*   **Learning Rates (Inference):**
-    *   Latent vectors $\mathbf{z_s}, \mathbf{z_t}$: $1 \times 10^{-2}$ [S2].
-    *   Camera azimuth $\phi$: $1 \times 10^{-2}$ [S2].
-    *   Camera elevation $\theta$: $1 \times 10^{-1}$ [S2].
-    *   Camera distance $\rho$: $1 \times 10^{-1}$ [S2].
-*   **Regularization Weight:** $1/\nu^2$ [S2].
-*   **Value of $\nu$:** Not explicitly given in the sources [S2].
-*   **Samples per Ray:** 64 [S2].
-*   **Rays per Image (Training):** 4094 [S2].
-*   **Inference Iterations:** 299 [S2].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Camera Initialization:** Initial camera pose estimates can have rotation errors above 30 degrees (average 80 degrees from ground truth) [S2]. The optimization must be robust to this; it converged in 85% of cases in experiments [S2].
-*   **Disentanglement Failure:** If the architecture is modified to input viewing direction into the shape network (M1) or use a single embedding for shape and texture (M2), disentanglement fails [S2]. M1 synthesizes incorrect textures, and M2 causes unwanted shape changes during texture editing [S2].
-*   **No 3D Supervision:** The model is trained end-to-end from images only, without 3D ground truth supervision, relying on multi-view photometric consistency [S2].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training involves optimizing network weights and latent codes for all objects in the category [S2]. Inference requires iterative optimization (299 iterations) per unseen object to find latent codes and camera pose [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing latent embeddings for each object in the training set ($M$ objects) [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On the ShapeNet-SRN benchmark, CodeNeRF achieves competitive PSNR and SSIM scores for novel view synthesis and few-shot reconstruction compared to methods like SRN, PixelNeRF, and GRF [S2].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Disentangles shape and texture, allowing independent editing [S2].
-    *   Does not require known camera pose at inference time [S2].
-    *   Generalizes well to unseen objects from a single image [S2].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires training on a category of objects first [S2].
-    *   Convergence is not guaranteed (85% success rate in pose estimation) [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires joint optimization of network weights and latent codes for all objects, which is computationally intensive. Inference requires iterative optimization (299 iterations) for each new object instance [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores latent codes for each object in the training set. The network size is comparable to standard NeRF but with two branches [S2].
+*   **Performance:** On the ShapeNet-SRN benchmark, CodeNeRF achieves competitive PSNR and SSIM scores for novel view synthesis and few-shot reconstruction, often outperforming methods that require known camera poses at test time [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Disentangles shape and texture, allowing independent editing. Does not require known camera poses at inference. Generalizes well to unseen objects [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Inference is slow due to iterative optimization. Performance depends on the quality of the learned category prior. Convergence of camera pose optimization is not guaranteed (85% convergence rate in experiments) [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When single-image 3D reconstruction of unseen objects from a known category is required, and explicit control over shape/texture is desired.
+*   **When not to use:** For scene-specific NeRFs where high-fidelity reconstruction of a single static scene is needed without editing requirements, or when real-time inference is critical [S2].
 
 ## Background
-CodeNeRF was introduced at ICCV 2021 [S2]. It builds on the original NeRF and takes inspiration from SRN and DeepSDF [S2]. It addresses the limitation of NeRF being scene-specific by learning a category-level representation [S2]. It is used for single-view reconstruction, novel view synthesis, and shape/texture editing on datasets like ShapeNet-SRN (cars and chairs) [S2].
+CodeNeRF was introduced by Jiang et al. in 2021 [S2]. It builds on the original NeRF [S2] and draws inspiration from SRN [S2] and DeepSDF [S2]. It addresses the limitation of NeRF being scene-specific by learning a category-level representation. The method was evaluated on the ShapeNet-SRN dataset for cars and chairs [S2].
 
